tel 1.0

base syndesis
import soundness

info   DNS-based service discovery over multicast DNS: advertise a service, browse for others, resolve an instance
detail
    _Syndesis_ lets a program which serves something on the local network — a build daemon, a
    printer, a game — be found without anyone being told its address. It implements
    **DNS-based service discovery** (RFC 6763), which names a service *type* (`_fury._tcp`)
    and its *instances* (`Elsinore._fury._tcp.local`), carried over **multicast DNS** (RFC
    6762), where every host answers for its own names and no nameserver is involved.

    The vocabulary is in `Discovery`: a `Discovery.Service` and a `Discovery.Instance`, which
    compute their DNS-SD names when constructed, under validation; a `Discovery.Txt` record of
    `key=value` pairs; a `Discovery.Description` of what an instance advertises; and a
    `Discovery.Resolution` of where an instance is. The three operations are extensions —
    `advertise` and `browse` on a service, `resolve` on an instance — each delegating to the
    `Discovery.Backend` in scope. The one backend provided is `Mdns.Responder`, the
    socket-based mDNS responder, selected by importing `discoveryBackends.mdnsSockets`.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import threads.platformThreads
    import probates.awaitProbate
    import abstractables.millisecondsAbstractable
    import socketBackends.javaBaseSockets
    import discoveryBackends.mdnsSockets

    val fury = Discovery.Service(t"fury", Tcp)
    val txt = Discovery.Txt(t"fingerprint" -> t"ab12")

    supervise:
      fury.advertise(Discovery.Description(t"Elsinore", tcp"8443", txt)):
        fury.browse:
          summon[Discovery.Browser].events.each:
            case Discovery.Event.Found(instance) => instance.resolve(5000L).endpoints
            case Discovery.Event.Lost(instance)  => ()
    ```

    An advertisement and a browse are **loans**: each lasts for its block and ends with it —
    or with the `Monitor` it runs under, whose cancellation unwinds the block. Both need a
    `Monitor^`, a `SharedProbate`, a `Tactic[Discovery.Error]` and a `Discovery.Activity is
    Loggable` in scope, besides the backend. The backend is an explicit `using` clause of its
    own, so a responder other than the given one can be named: `fury.browse(using other)`.

    The library has one source set, `syndesis.core`, for the JVM. `Mdns` is both the
    responder and its parts — the `Mdns.Transport` seam it speaks through, the `Mdns.Cache`
    of what it has heard, and `Mdns.Tiebreak` — of which `Mdns.Transport.Bus`, an in-memory
    link, is the one a program under test needs: responders joined to one bus hear one
    another and nothing else, so the protocol runs deterministically without a network.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, and the `discoveryBackends` choice
    package merges with the umbrella's own; `import soundness.*` brings in no backend, so
    `mdnsSockets` is imported by name.

refer syndesis.Discovery
refer syndesis.Mdns
refer syndesis.advertise
refer syndesis.browse
refer syndesis.resolve

entry .Discovery   the vocabulary of DNS-SD: services, instances, TXT records, and the backend seam
  detail
      `Discovery` holds everything a program says and hears about services, independently of
      how the names travel. A `Service` is a type such as `_fury._tcp`; an `Instance` is one
      named provider of it; a `Txt` record is what an instance says about itself; a
      `Description` is what an instance advertises; a `Resolution` is where a found instance
      turned out to be. The `Backend` trait is the seam a transport implements, and
      `Advertisement` and `Browser` are the handles the `advertise` and `browse` loans lend.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val fury = Discovery.Service(t"fury", Tcp)
      val elsinore = Discovery.Instance(t"Elsinore", fury)
      fury.dnsName                         // dns"_fury._tcp.local"
      elsinore.show                          // t"Elsinore._fury._tcp.local"
      Discovery.Txt(t"fp" -> t"abc").strings   // List(t"fp=abc")
      ```

      Every name is computed when its value is constructed, under validation, so a name over
      the DNS limits is a `Discovery.Error` at construction rather than a failure on the wire,
      and every later use of the name is total.
  refer syndesis.Discovery.Service
  refer syndesis.Discovery.Instance
  refer syndesis.Discovery.Backend
  refer syndesis.Discovery.Error
  entry .local   the `local` domain, `dns"local"`, under which every mDNS name sits
  entry .Service   a service type, such as `_fury._tcp`: a registered name and a transport
    detail
        A service type is a registered name of up to fifteen characters (RFC 6763 §7),
        without its leading underscore, and a protocol which is either `Tcp` or `Udp`. Its
        `#dnsName` is `_name._tcp.local` (or `_udp`), computed and validated at construction.
        An optional list of `#subtypes` names narrower kinds of the service, each browsed under
        `_subtype._sub._name._tcp.local` (`#subtypeName`), and each advertised instance is
        listed under every subtype of its service.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val fury = Discovery.Service(t"fury", Tcp)
        fury.dnsName                         // dns"_fury._tcp.local"
        fury.show                            // t"_fury._tcp"
        fury.subtypeName(t"printer")         // dns"_printer._sub._fury._tcp.local"
        Discovery.Service(t"sip", Udp).dnsName   // dns"_sip._udp.local"
        ```

        A name which is empty, longer than fifteen characters or begins with `_` is rejected
        with `Discovery.Error` reason `InvalidService`. The constructor is private: a service
        is made with `Service.apply` or read back from a DNS name with `Service.parse`.
    entry .enumeration   the meta-query name every service type on the link is listed under
        `dns"_services._dns-sd._udp.local"` (RFC 6763 §9): the responder advertises a PTR from
        this name to each service type it has an instance of, so a browser can enumerate the
        types on the link before choosing one.
    entry .apply   a validated service type from its name, protocol and optional subtypes
        `Discovery.Service(name, Tcp | Udp, subtypes = Nil)`. Needs a
        `Tactic[Discovery.Error]`; raises reason `InvalidService` for an empty name, one over
        fifteen characters, or one beginning with `_`, and `InvalidName` if the resulting DNS
        name breaks a DNS limit.
    entry .parse   the service type a DNS name such as `_fury._tcp.local` denotes, or `Unset`
        The inverse of `#dnsName`. The name must have exactly three labels, the last `local`,
        the second `_tcp` or `_udp`, and the first beginning with `_`; anything else is `Unset`.
        A parsed service has no subtypes.
    entry .showable   renders a service type in its registered form, `_fury._tcp`
    entry #name   the registered service name, without its leading underscore
    entry #protocol   the transport, `Tcp` or `Udp`
    entry #subtypes   the subtypes an instance of this service is also listed under
    entry #dnsName   the DNS-SD name of the service type: `_fury._tcp.local`
    entry #subtypeName   the name a subtype's instances are browsed under
        `_printer._sub._fury._tcp.local` for the subtype `printer`. Needs a
        `Tactic[Discovery.Error]`, raising `InvalidName` if the label breaks a DNS limit.
  entry .Instance   one named provider of a service: a UTF-8 label under the service type
    detail
        An instance is a single DNS label of UTF-8 (RFC 6763 §4.1.1) — spaces and dots
        included, no escaping, up to 63 bytes — prefixed to its service's name, so
        `Instance(t"Hamlet's Printer. Upstairs", fury)` has the labels `Hamlet's Printer. Upstairs`,
        `_fury`, `_tcp`, `local`. It shows in the presentation form of §4.3, with the label's
        dots escaped: `Hamlet\.Printer._fury._tcp.local`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val fury = Discovery.Service(t"fury", Tcp)
        val elsinore = Discovery.Instance(t"Elsinore", fury)
        elsinore.renamed.label                   // t"Elsinore (2)"
        Discovery.Instance.parse(dns"Elsinore._fury._tcp.local") == elsinore
        ```

        An instance is the value a browser reports as found or lost, and the one `resolve` is
        called on. The constructor is private: an instance is made with `Instance.apply` or
        read back from a PTR record's target with `Instance.parse`.
    entry .apply   a validated instance from its label and service
        Needs a `Tactic[Discovery.Error]`; an empty label, or one over 63 bytes, raises reason
        `InvalidName` naming the label.
    entry .parse   the instance a name such as `Elsinore._fury._tcp.local` denotes, or `Unset`
        The first label is the instance's label, kept exactly as written, and the rest must
        parse as a `Service`; a name with no labels, or whose parent is not a service type
        (such as the service type's own name), is `Unset`.
    entry .showable   renders an instance as its DNS name, with the label's dots escaped
    entry #label   the instance's own name, one label of UTF-8
    entry #service   the service type the instance provides
    entry #dnsName   the full DNS-SD name: the label, then the service's name
    entry #renamed   the next name to try after a conflict: `Elsinore` → `Elsinore (2)` → `Elsinore (3)`
        RFC 6762 §9's renaming: a counter in parentheses is appended, or incremented if the
        label already ends with one. Needs a `Tactic[Discovery.Error]`, since the new label is
        validated like any other. The responder renames an advertised instance this way when
        another host holds, or wins, its name.
  entry .Txt   an instance's TXT record: `key=value` pairs, read case-insensitively, first occurrence winning
    detail
        A TXT record is a list of entries, each a key with an optional value (RFC 6763 §6).
        Keys are printable ASCII without `=`, and each `key=value` string must fit the
        255-octet character-string. Duplicate keys are kept as written, but `#apply` and
        `#has` read the first occurrence of a key, comparing keys without regard to ASCII case,
        as §6.4 says a receiver should.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val txt = Discovery.Txt(t"fp" -> t"abc", t"flag" -> Unset)
        txt(t"FP")           // t"abc"
        txt(t"flag")         // Unset, though present
        txt.has(t"flag")     // true
        txt.strings          // List(t"fp=abc", t"flag")
        ```

        A `Txt` travels in a `Description` when advertising and comes back in a `Resolution`.
    entry .empty   the TXT record with no entries, which the wire carries as one empty string
    entry .apply   a validated TXT record from `key -> value` pairs, a value being `Optional[Text]`
        Needs a `Tactic[Discovery.Error]`; a key which is empty, contains `=` or a
        non-printable character, or whose `key=value` string exceeds 255 bytes of UTF-8,
        raises reason `InvalidTxt` naming the key.
    entry .parse   a TXT record from the strings of a received record
        Each string is `key=value`, or `key` alone for a key with no value (`Unset`); the
        first `=` splits, so `eq=a=b` is the key `eq` with the value `a=b`. The empty string
        which stands for an empty record is skipped. Nothing is validated.
    entry .showable   renders the record as its strings, each in double quotes, space-separated
    entry #entries   the entries in order, each a key and an optional value, duplicates kept
    entry #apply   the value of a key, or `Unset` if the key is absent or has no value
        `txt(key)` finds the first entry whose key matches without regard to ASCII case. A
        key present without a value and a key absent both give `Unset`; `#has` tells them
        apart.
    entry #has   whether a key is present at all, with or without a value
    entry #strings   the record's wire strings: one per entry, or a single empty string for none
  entry .Description   what an instance advertises: its name, port, TXT record, host, priority and weight
    detail
        The input to `advertise`: the label the instance asks for (which a conflict may
        rename), the port it serves on (a `Port`, so `tcp"8443"` or `udp"5004"`), its `Txt`
        record, and optionally the host name its address records are published under — by
        default this host's own name — and the SRV record's `priority` and `weight`, for a
        service with several instances.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val txt = Discovery.Txt(t"fp" -> t"abc")
        Discovery.Description(t"Elsinore", tcp"8443", txt)
        ```
    entry #instance   the label asked for; the name claimed may differ after a conflict
    entry #port   the port the instance serves on
    entry #txt   the TXT record to publish; `Txt.empty` by default
    entry #host   the host name to publish address records under, or `Unset` for this host's
    entry #priority   the SRV record's priority, `0` by default
    entry #weight   the SRV record's weight, `0` by default
  entry .Resolution   where a found instance is and what it says: host, port, TXT and addresses
    detail
        The result of `resolve`: the instance, the DNS name of the host serving it, the port
        (typed to the service's transport), its `Txt` record, and the host's IPv4 and IPv6
        addresses. `#endpoints` pairs each address with the port, ready to connect to in turn.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val fury = Discovery.Service(t"fury", Tcp)
        val elsinore = Discovery.Instance(t"Elsinore", fury)

        val resolution =
          Discovery.Resolution
            (elsinore, dns"elsinore.local", tcp"8443", Discovery.Txt.empty, List(ip"192.168.1.2", ip"fe80::1"))

        resolution.endpoints.map(_.remote)   // List(t"192.168.1.2", t"fe80::1")
        ```
    entry #instance   the instance which was resolved
    entry #host   the DNS name of the host serving the instance, from its SRV record
    entry #port   the port, from the SRV record
    entry #txt   the instance's TXT record
    entry #addresses   the host's addresses, IPv4 then IPv6, as `Ipv4 | Ipv6` values
    entry #endpoints   each address paired with the port, as an `Endpoint[Port]`
  entry .Event   what a browser sees: an instance found on the link, or lost from it
    detail
        `Found(instance)` is an instance appearing — its PTR record arriving in the cache —
        and `Lost(instance)` is one leaving, by a goodbye or by its records expiring. A change
        to an instance's details (its port or TXT record) is not an event: resolve it again.
        A `Browser` delivers these through `#events`.
    entry .showable   renders an event as `found Elsinore._fury._tcp.local` or `lost …`
  entry .Activity   what a backend reports as it works, logged through the `Loggable` at each loan
    detail
        Each loan — an advertisement, a browse, a resolution — takes a `Discovery.Activity is
        Loggable`, to which the backend reports what it does for that loan: `Probing(name)`
        and `Yielded(name)` at `fine` level and `Conflicted(name)` while a name is being
        claimed; `Claimed(instance)` and `Withdrawn(instance)` as an advertisement starts and
        ends; `Browsing(service)` and `Resolved(instance)`. All are tagged `Log.Network`, and
        the probing ones `Log.Protocol` as well. A backend holds no logger of its own, so its
        background work between loans — defending a name, re-probing after a conflict — goes
        unreported; `logging.silentLogging` satisfies the requirement when nothing is wanted.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        given loggable: Discovery.Activity is Loggable = (_, _, event) => ()
        ```
    entry .silent   a `Loggable` which discards every activity, for a backend's background work
    entry .communicable   renders each activity as a message: _probing for the name `…`_, _claimed and announced `…`_, …
  entry .Backend   what a transport does for discovery: advertise, withdraw, browse, dismiss, resolve
    detail
        The seam behind `advertise`, `browse` and `resolve`: each loan calls a start and, in a
        `finally`, the matching stop. Each start takes the `Monitor^` under which the backend
        runs its background tasks (so cancelling the monitor ends them), a `SharedProbate`, a
        `Tactic[Discovery.Error]` and the `Loggable` the loan's `Activity` goes to. A backend
        holds no capabilities of its own, which is what lets loans nest — a browse inside an
        advertisement. `Mdns.Responder` is the implementation provided; the given
        `discoveryBackends.mdnsSockets` builds one over the `Socket.Backend` in scope.

        The intent is one backend per program: bind it once as a `given` rather than summoning
        it afresh at each use, which would open a socket per summons.
    entry #advertise   claims a name for the instance and announces it, yielding the running `Advertising`
        Probes for the name, renames on conflict, and announces; the `Advertising` returned
        reads the instance currently claimed, which a later conflict may rename again. Raises
        `Discovery.Error` reason `Conflict` if no name can be claimed, `InvalidTxt` if the TXT
        record cannot be encoded, and `Unavailable` if the transport cannot be opened.
    entry #withdraw   ends an advertisement: sends its goodbye and releases the backend's loan
    entry #browse   starts a browse of a service type, yielding the `Browsing` whose relay carries events
    entry #dismiss   ends a browse: stops its re-query loop and its relay
    entry #resolve   resolves an instance within a timeout, raising `Discovery.Error` reason `Timeout` otherwise
        The timeout is any duration `Abstractable across Durations to Long`, in milliseconds
        (a plain `5000L` with `abstractables.millisecondsAbstractable` imported).
  entry .Browsing   a browse as a backend hands it over: its event relay and a snapshot of its instances
    detail
        A plain value, built by a backend's `browse`, which the `browse` loan wraps in the
        `Browser` it lends. A program uses `Browser`; a backend author constructs this.
    entry #relay   the `Relay[Event]` the backend puts found and lost events on
    entry #instances   the instances the backend currently knows, as a `Set`
  entry .Advertising   an advertisement as a backend hands it over: the instance currently claimed
    detail
        The trait a backend's `advertise` returns and its `withdraw` takes back, wrapped by
        the loan in the `Advertisement` it lends. `#instance` is read live: a conflict after
        establishment may rename it.
    entry #instance   the instance currently claimed, after any renaming
  entry .Advertisement   the handle an `advertise` loan lends: the instance as currently claimed
    detail
        Available inside an `advertise` block as `summon[Discovery.Advertisement]`. Its
        `#instance` is read live rather than copied, because a host which turns up later
        asserting the same name makes the responder probe afresh and, if the probe is
        answered, rename: `Elsinore` may become `Elsinore (2)` during the block. The handle is a
        plain value, not a capability — the socket and the background tasks belong to the
        `Monitor` — which is what lets it pass through a nested loan.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import threads.platformThreads
        import probates.awaitProbate
        import socketBackends.javaBaseSockets
        import discoveryBackends.mdnsSockets

        val fury = Discovery.Service(t"fury", Tcp)

        supervise:
          fury.advertise(Discovery.Description(t"Elsinore", tcp"8443")):
            summon[Discovery.Advertisement].instance.label   // t"Elsinore", or t"Elsinore (2)"
        ```
    entry #instance   the instance currently claimed, which a conflict may have renamed
  entry .Browser   the handle a `browse` loan lends: events as they happen, and the instances known
    detail
        Available inside a `browse` block as `summon[Discovery.Browser]`. `#events` is a
        `Chain` of `Event`s, delivered as instances appear and leave — instances already known
        when the browse began are reported first, as `Found` — and `#instances` is the set
        currently known. The chain ends when the loan does.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import threads.platformThreads
        import probates.awaitProbate
        import socketBackends.javaBaseSockets
        import discoveryBackends.mdnsSockets

        val fury = Discovery.Service(t"fury", Tcp)

        supervise:
          fury.browse:
            summon[Discovery.Browser].events.head   // the first Found or Lost event
        ```
    entry #events   the `Chain[Event]` of instances found and lost, as they happen
    entry #instances   the instances currently known, as a `Set[Instance]`
  entry .Error   raised when a name is invalid, cannot be claimed, does not resolve, or no backend starts
    detail
        Error `SN-054`: _service discovery failed because `reason`_. The `#reason` is one of
        `Discovery.Error.Reason`'s cases, numbered 1 to 6:

        ```tabular
        column  Case
        column  Code
        column  Message
        column  Note
        row  `Conflict(name)`  1  _the name `name` could not be claimed after repeated conflicts_  after a hundred renamings
        row  `Timeout(instance)`  2  _the instance `instance` did not resolve before the timeout_
        row  `InvalidName(text)`  3  _the name `text` is not a valid DNS-SD name_  a label or name is empty, over 63 bytes, or over a DNS limit
        row  `InvalidTxt(key)`  4  _the TXT entry `key` is not valid_  its key is empty, contains `=` or a non-printable character, or the entry exceeds 255 bytes
        row  `InvalidService(name)`  5  _the service name `name` is not valid_  empty, over fifteen characters, or beginning with `_`
        row  `Unavailable`  6  _no service discovery backend could be started_  the transport could not be opened; the responder's own `Mdns.Error` is the cause
        ```

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        capture[Discovery.Error](Discovery.Service(t"_fury", Tcp)).reason
        // Discovery.Error.Reason.InvalidService(t"_fury")
        ```
    entry .Reason   why discovery failed: `Conflict`, `Timeout`, `InvalidName`, `InvalidTxt`, `InvalidService` or `Unavailable`
      detail
          An enum, each case numbered, with a `Communicable` rendering such as _the name
          `Elsinore._fury._tcp.local` could not be claimed after repeated conflicts_ or _the
          instance `…` did not resolve before the timeout_.
      entry .communicable   renders each reason as a message
      entry #number   the reason's number, `1` to `6`, the second part of the error's code
    entry #reason   why discovery failed

entry .advertise   `service.advertise(description) { … }`: advertises an instance for the block
  detail
      An extension on `Discovery.Service`. The instance is probed for, claimed — renamed
      `Elsinore (2)` if another host holds the name, or this responder already advertises it —
      and announced; it stays advertised for the block, and a goodbye is sent when the block
      ends, normally or by an exception, or when the `Monitor` is cancelled. The block
      receives a `Discovery.Advertisement`, from which the name actually claimed is read.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import threads.platformThreads
      import probates.awaitProbate
      import socketBackends.javaBaseSockets
      import discoveryBackends.mdnsSockets

      val fury = Discovery.Service(t"fury", Tcp)
      val description = Discovery.Description(t"Elsinore", tcp"8443", Discovery.Txt(t"fp" -> t"abc"))

      supervise:
        fury.advertise(description):
          summon[Discovery.Advertisement].instance
      ```

      The backend is a `using` clause of its own, before the block, so one other than the
      given can be named: `fury.advertise(description)(using responder) { … }`. The block
      also needs a `Monitor^` (from `supervise`), a `SharedProbate`, a
      `Tactic[Discovery.Error]` and a `Discovery.Activity is Loggable`. Raises
      `Discovery.Error` for an invalid label or TXT record, a name which cannot be claimed
      after repeated conflicts, or a transport which cannot be opened. Loans nest: a browse
      may run inside an advertisement.
  refer syndesis.Discovery.Advertisement
  refer syndesis.Discovery.Description
  refer syndesis.browse

entry .browse   `service.browse { … }`: watches for instances of a service type for the block
  detail
      An extension on `Discovery.Service`. The block receives a `Discovery.Browser`, whose
      `events` are the instances appearing on and leaving the link, as they happen, and whose
      `instances` are those currently known. Instances already in the cache are reported at
      once as `Found`; then the service type is queried, and queried again at intervals
      doubling from a second to an hour, each query telling other responders what is already
      known. The browse ends with the block, or with the `Monitor`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import threads.platformThreads
      import probates.awaitProbate
      import abstractables.millisecondsAbstractable
      import socketBackends.javaBaseSockets
      import discoveryBackends.mdnsSockets

      val fury = Discovery.Service(t"fury", Tcp)

      supervise:
        fury.browse:
          summon[Discovery.Browser].events.each:
            case Discovery.Event.Found(instance) => instance.resolve(5000L).txt(t"fp")
            case Discovery.Event.Lost(instance)  => ()
      ```

      The backend is a `using` clause of its own (`fury.browse(using responder) { … }`), and
      the block needs a `Monitor^`, a `SharedProbate`, a `Tactic[Discovery.Error]` and a
      `Discovery.Activity is Loggable`. The browse logs `Discovery.Activity.Browsing`.
  refer syndesis.Discovery.Browser
  refer syndesis.Discovery.Event
  refer syndesis.resolve

entry .resolve   `instance.resolve(timeout)`: the instance's host, port, TXT record and addresses
  detail
      An extension on `Discovery.Instance`, giving a `Discovery.Resolution`. If the cache
      already holds the instance's SRV and TXT records and its host's address records, the
      resolution is immediate; otherwise those records are queried for and the call waits up
      to the timeout for them to arrive. The timeout is any value `Abstractable across
      Durations to Long`, in milliseconds — a `5000L` with
      `abstractables.millisecondsAbstractable` imported, or an aviation duration.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import threads.platformThreads
      import probates.awaitProbate
      import abstractables.millisecondsAbstractable
      import socketBackends.javaBaseSockets
      import discoveryBackends.mdnsSockets

      val fury = Discovery.Service(t"fury", Tcp)
      val elsinore = Discovery.Instance(t"Elsinore", fury)

      supervise:
        val resolution = elsinore.resolve(5000L)
        (resolution.port.number, resolution.txt(t"fp"), resolution.endpoints)
      ```

      Raises `Discovery.Error` reason `Timeout` if the records do not arrive in time, naming
      the instance. The call is a loan on the backend like the others — it takes the backend
      as its own `using` clause, and needs a `Monitor^`, a `SharedProbate`, a
      `Tactic[Discovery.Error]` and a `Discovery.Activity is Loggable` — and logs
      `Discovery.Activity.Resolved` on success.
  refer syndesis.Discovery.Resolution
  refer syndesis.Discovery.Instance

entry .discoveryBackends   the discovery backends, each a given to import by name
  detail
      Which backend advertises, browses and resolves is the `Discovery.Backend` given in
      scope. `import soundness.*` brings none in: one is imported by name, and the intent is
      one backend per program, bound once.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import socketBackends.javaBaseSockets
      import discoveryBackends.mdnsSockets
      ```

      For a program under test, an `Mdns.Responder` over an `Mdns.Transport.Bus` can be
      bound as a local given, or passed explicitly as `(using responder)`, in place of the
      socket-based one.
  entry .mdnsSockets   the mDNS responder over the multicast sockets of the `Socket.Backend` in scope
      An `Mdns.Responder` whose transport is `Mdns.Transport.sockets` over the
      `Socket.Backend` given (such as `socketBackends.javaBaseSockets`), with multicast
      loopback, a multicast hop limit of 255, a datagram size of 9000 bytes, and whatever
      `Socket.Option.Udp`s are in scope as an `Every`. It joins `224.0.0.251:5353` and
      `[ff02::fb]:5353` on every interface which is up, multicast-capable and has an address
      of the group's family, with address and port reuse, so it runs beside the system's own
      responder. The sockets are opened at the first loan and closed after the last; a host
      with no multicast-capable interface fails the first loan with `Discovery.Error` reason
      `Unavailable`.

      The given is computed each time it is summoned, so bind it once — `given backend:
      Discovery.Backend = discoveryBackends.mdnsSockets` — rather than summoning it at each
      use, which would open a socket per summons.

entry .Mdns   the multicast DNS responder (RFC 6762) and its parts: transport, cache, tiebreaking
  detail
      `Mdns` is the one `Discovery.Backend` provided: `Mdns.Responder`, a responder which
      speaks through an `Mdns.Transport` — the sockets, or an in-memory `Mdns.Transport.Bus`
      — keeps what it hears in an `Mdns.Cache`, and settles simultaneous probes with
      `Mdns.Tiebreak`. The transport is a seam of its own, below `Discovery`'s, so the protocol
      runs identically over the network and over a bus, which is how it is tested without
      touching the host's network.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import threads.platformThreads
      import probates.awaitProbate
      import abstractables.millisecondsAbstractable

      val fury = Discovery.Service(t"fury", Tcp)
      val description = Discovery.Description(t"Elsinore", tcp"8443")

      supervise:
        val bus = Mdns.Transport.Bus()
        val a = Mdns.Responder(() => bus.join(dns"a.local", List(ip"10.0.0.1")))
        val b = Mdns.Responder(() => bus.join(dns"b.local", List(ip"10.0.0.2")))

        fury.advertise(description)(using a):
          fury.browse(using b):
            summon[Discovery.Browser].events.head   // Discovery.Event.Found(Elsinore._fury._tcp.local)
      ```

      The responder's own failure to start — no multicast-capable interface, or a group it
      cannot join — is an `Mdns.Error`, which the loan reports as `Discovery.Error` reason
      `Unavailable`.
  refer syndesis.Mdns.Responder
  refer syndesis.Mdns.Transport
  refer syndesis.Discovery.Backend
  entry .group4   the IPv4 mDNS group, `224.0.0.251` on port 5353
  entry .group6   the IPv6 mDNS group, `ff02::fb` on port 5353
  entry .port   the mDNS port, `udp"mdns"` (5353), the source and destination of every message
  entry .Transport   what the responder needs of the network: this host's name and addresses, send, reply, receive, close
    detail
        The seam between the protocol and the link. A transport publishes this host's
        `#host` name and `#addresses` (what its own address records say), sends a message to
        the group, sends a unicast reply, offers `#inlets` to receive from, and closes. Two
        implementations: `Transport.sockets`, over a `Socket.Backend`, and `Transport.Bus`, an
        in-memory link for tests. `Mdns.Responder` takes a function which opens one, so the
        transport is opened at the first loan and closed after the last.
    entry .Inlet   one source of packets, received one at a time by a blocking call
      detail
          A joined socket, in practice: a transport over both groups has two, and the
          responder reads each in a loop of its own. A `Bus` member is its own single inlet,
          which a test may receive from directly.
      entry #receive   the next packet, blocking until one arrives; raises `Socket.Error` once closed
    entry .sockets   a transport over the multicast sockets of a `Socket.Backend`
        `Mdns.Transport.sockets(backend, options)` joins the IPv4 group on every suitable
        interface with an IPv4 address and the IPv6 group on every one with an IPv6 address —
        the IPv6 group is left out on an IPv4-only host — and fails with `Mdns.Error` reason
        `NoInterface` if no interface is up and multicast-capable, or reason `Join` if neither
        group can be joined. This host's name is the first label of its hostname under
        `.local` (or `soundness.local` if it has none), and its addresses are those of the
        joined interfaces. A unicast reply leaves through the socket of the destination's
        family. `discoveryBackends.mdnsSockets` builds a responder over this.
    entry .Bus   an in-memory link: every member receives what any sends, itself included
      detail
          A `Bus` stands in for the network under test. Each `#join` adds a member with a host
          name and addresses; a member's `send` is delivered to every member's queue, the
          sender included (as multicast loopback does), and a `reply` reaches the member whose
          addresses include the destination. A member is both a `Transport` and its own
          `Inlet`, so a test can `receive()` from it directly to watch the packets a responder
          emits, and `close()` makes a pending receive fail with `Socket.Error`.

          ```scala
          import soundness.*
          import strategies.throwUnsafely

          val bus = Mdns.Transport.Bus()
          val first = bus.join(dns"first.local", List(ip"10.0.0.1"))
          val second = bus.join(dns"second.local", List(ip"10.0.0.2"))
          first.send(Data(1, 2, 3))
          second.receive().sender   // ip"10.0.0.1"
          ```
      entry #join   adds a member with a host name and addresses, as a `Transport & Transport.Inlet`
    entry #host   this host's name, as its address records publish it
    entry #addresses   this host's addresses, which its A and AAAA records carry
    entry #send   multicasts a message to the group; raises `Socket.Error`
    entry #reply   sends a unicast reply to an address and port; raises `Socket.Error`
    entry #inlets   the inlets to receive from, one per joined socket
    entry #close   closes the transport, making any pending receive fail
  entry .Cache   the records heard on the link, by name, type and data, honouring their TTLs
    detail
        The responder's memory of what other hosts have said, keyed by a record's identity
        (name, type and data) and kept in the monotonic nanoseconds of `System.nanoTime`. It
        applies RFC 6762 §10: a record heard again is refreshed; a goodbye (TTL 0) lapses a
        second later; a record with the cache-flush bit retires the other records of its
        name and type which are more than a second old. What absorbing or sweeping changed
        comes back as `Cache.Transition`s, which the responder turns into browse events.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val second = 1_000_000_000L
        val record = Dns.Record(dns"elsinore.local", 120, Dns.Rdata.A(ip"10.0.0.1"))
        val cache = Mdns.Cache()
        cache.absorb(List(record), 0L)        // List(Mdns.Cache.Transition.Added(record))
        cache.sweep(121*second)               // List(Mdns.Cache.Transition.Removed(record))
        ```
    entry .Entry   what the cache holds for one record: when it arrived and when it lapses
      entry #record   the record
      entry #received   when it was last heard, in `System.nanoTime` nanoseconds
      entry #expiry   when it lapses, in the same clock
    entry .Transition   what absorbing or sweeping changed: `Added`, `Refreshed` or `Removed`, each with its record
      detail
          `Added(record)` is a record not previously held, `Refreshed(record)` one heard
          again, and `Removed(record)` one which `#sweep` found lapsed. A PTR record of a
          browsed service type added or removed is an instance found or lost.
      entry #record   the record the transition concerns
    entry #absorb   takes in received records at a time, reporting what was added or refreshed
    entry #sweep   drops every record lapsed by the given time, reporting each as `Removed`
    entry #lookup   the unexpired records of a name and type at the given time
    entry #knownAnswers   the records of a name and type with over half their TTL left, with the TTL remaining
        What a query tells other responders it already knows (RFC 6762 §7.1), so they need
        not answer again.
    entry #earliestExpiry   when the first record of a name and type lapses, or `Unset` if none is held
    entry #size   how many records the cache holds
  entry .Tiebreak   the order in which two hosts' simultaneous probes for one name are compared
    detail
        RFC 6762 §8.2.1: when two hosts probe for the same name at once, each compares the
        records it proposes with the other's, and the one whose records are lexicographically
        lower yields. A record ranks by class, then type, then its uncompressed data as
        unsigned bytes, a prefix ranking below what extends it. Two sets of records are
        sorted and compared pairwise: the first difference decides, a set which runs out first
        loses, and identical sets are `Comparison.Same` — no conflict at all.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val a = Dns.Record(dns"host.local", 120, Dns.Rdata.A(ip"10.0.0.1"), flush = true)
        val srv = Dns.Record(dns"Elsinore._fury._tcp.local", 120, Dns.Rdata.Srv(0, 0, 8443, dns"host.local"), flush = true)
        Mdns.Tiebreak.compare(a, srv)                   // Comparison.Less: A before SRV
        Mdns.Tiebreak.compare(List(srv, a), List(a, srv))   // Comparison.Same
        ```
    entry .compare   compares two records, or two lists of proposed records, as `Comparison`
  entry .Responder   the mDNS responder: one per program, serving every loan through one transport
    detail
        `Mdns.Responder(open)` takes a function which opens an `Mdns.Transport`; the transport
        is opened at the first loan and closed when the last ends, and its receive loops and
        cache sweeper run as tasks under the `Monitor` the loan supplies, so cancelling that
        monitor ends them too. It is a `Discovery.Backend`, so it is what `advertise`,
        `browse` and `resolve` call; `discoveryBackends.mdnsSockets` is one over the sockets,
        and one over an `Mdns.Transport.Bus` is how a program is tested.

        Of RFC 6762 it does: probing three times at 250 ms, with renaming on conflict and
        tiebreaking between simultaneous probes; announcing twice, a second apart; defending
        an established name, and re-probing it (renaming if the probe is answered) when a
        later response conflicts with it; answering queries, with known-answer suppression, a
        random 20–120 ms delay for shared records, answers falling due together aggregated
        into one message, and legacy unicast replies for a querier not on port 5353; goodbyes;
        TTL-honouring browsing with exponential re-query; and resolving. Its own echoes are
        recognised by message ID.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import threads.platformThreads
        import probates.awaitProbate

        val fury = Discovery.Service(t"fury", Tcp)
        val description = Discovery.Description(t"Elsinore", tcp"8443")

        supervise:
          val bus = Mdns.Transport.Bus()
          val a = Mdns.Responder(() => bus.join(dns"a.local", List(ip"10.0.0.1")))

          fury.advertise(description)(using a):
            fury.advertise(description.copy(port = tcp"8444"))(using a):
              summon[Discovery.Advertisement].instance.label   // t"Elsinore (2)"
        ```
    refer syndesis.Discovery.Backend
    refer syndesis.Mdns.Transport
    entry #advertise   probes for, claims and announces an instance, as `Discovery.Backend#advertise`
        Publishes the PTR from the service type (and from the enumeration meta-type, and from
        each subtype), the SRV and TXT records, and the host's address records; the last three
        are unique to the instance, so they carry the cache-flush bit. PTRs live 75 minutes,
        the rest two minutes. A name this responder already holds is renamed without probing.
    entry #withdraw   sends the goodbye for an advertisement and releases the loan
    entry #browse   starts a browse, reporting known instances at once and re-querying at doubling intervals
    entry #dismiss   stops a browse's re-query loop and its relay, and releases the loan
    entry #resolve   resolves an instance from the cache, or queries for its records and waits up to the timeout
  entry .Error   raised when the responder cannot start: no interface, or a group it cannot join
    detail
        Error `SN-055`: _the mDNS responder could not start because `reason`_. Reason
        `NoInterface` (2) is _no interface is up and capable of multicast_; reason
        `Join(group)` (1) is _the multicast group `224.0.0.251:5353` could not be joined_,
        raised when neither group could be joined. Raised by `Mdns.Transport.sockets`; a loan
        which hits it reports `Discovery.Error` reason `Unavailable` instead, with this error
        as its diagnostics.
    entry .Reason   why the responder could not start: `Join(group)` or `NoInterface`
      detail
          An enum of two numbered cases, with a `Communicable` rendering each.
      entry .communicable   renders each reason as a message
      entry #number   the reason's number, `1` or `2`, the second part of the error's code
    entry #reason   why the responder could not start
